+# Primary sources for the disparate-domain connections
+
+Accessed through Exa search/fetch on 12 November 2026. Ten successful searches requested five results each, yielding 50 candidate search-result entries, including duplicates; these were screened rather than counted as 50 fully read papers. Eight selected sources were fetched. Fetch output sometimes contains a bounded extract; claims below are limited to the definitions and arguments visible in the retrieved material, plus explicitly written deductions in the accompanying report. One earlier search attempt failed tool-schema validation and produced no usable source.
+
+| ID | Primary source | Verified support and scope |
+|---|---|---|
+| S1 | Yuan Zhang, Tyrone Fernando and Mohamed Darouach, [Functional Observability, Structural Functional Observability and Optimal Sensor Placement](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.08923), 2024 revision; [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1109/TAC.2024.3462558) | Functional observability concerns a specified state functional; output-history observability matrix; general-system qualifications; optimal sensor-selection complexity. The finite-horizon discrete field-matrix derivation in this report is written independently. |
+| S2 | Roberto Giacobazzi and Francesco Ranzato, [Completeness in Abstract Interpretation: A Domain Perspective](https://www.math.unipd.it/~ranzato/papers/amast97.pdf), AMAST 1997 | Distinguishes soundness from completeness and states the full-completeness equation `alpha ◦ T = T# ◦ alpha`; studies complete restrictions/extensions. The floor-division period criterion and proposed CRAM compiler are deductions here. |
+| S3 | Alan Nash, Luc Segoufin and Victor Vianu, [Views and Queries: Determinacy and Rewriting](https://lsv.ens-paris-saclay.fr/Publis/PAPERS/PDF/NSV-tods10.pdf), ACM TODS 35(3), Article 21, 2010; [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/1806907.1806913) | Defines determinacy as equality of views implying equality of query answers; separates information-theoretic determinacy from effective/language-specific rewriting. Explicitly discusses caching and public/secret query relationships. |
+| S4 | Rathinakumar Appuswamy, Massimo Franceschetti, Nikhil Karamchandani and Kenneth Zeger, [Linear Codes, Target Function Classes, and Network Computing Capacity](https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.0085), v3, 2011 | Network function computation differs from reproducing all messages; treats classes of target functions and linear/nonlinear coding under topology and capacity constraints. The consumer-specific Hamming-distance result in this report is an elementary independent derivation, not attributed as a new theorem of that paper. |
+| S5 | Marc Shapiro, Nuno Preguiça, Carlos Baquero and Marek Zawirski, [Convergent and Commutative Replicated Data Types](https://inria.hal.science/hal-00932833/document), Bulletin of the EATCS 104, 67–88, 2011 | Gives formal conditions for convergent state-based and commutative operation-based replicated objects; semilattice and monotonicity distinctions. [Companion INRIA report RR-7506](https://inria.hal.science/inria-00555588v1/document) was discovered in search. The modular/max obstruction and quotient-merge law are deductions here. |
+| S6 | Amit Singer, [Angular Synchronization by Eigenvectors and Semidefinite Programming](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3003935/), Applied and Computational Harmonic Analysis 30(1), 20–36, 2011; [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.acha.2010.02.001) | Defines pairwise phase synchronization, exact recovery up to a common phase on a connected graph, ambiguity across disconnected components, and separate noisy-estimation methods. The finite-group phase-lock return path is proposed here. |
+| S7 | David Blackwell, [Equivalent Comparisons of Experiments](https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177729032), Annals of Mathematical Statistics, 1953 | Original statistical comparison-of-experiments framework. Retrieved publisher/index abstract establishes subject and bibliographic provenance; this report does not claim a full independent audit of Blackwell's proof. The finite deterministic factorization and Bernoulli-sum example are proved directly in the report. |
+| S8 | John G. Kemeny and J. Laurie Snell, [Finite Markov Chains, §§6.3–6.4](https://math.pku.edu.cn/teachers/yaoy/Fall2011/Kemeny-Snell_Chapter6.3-4.pdf), 1976 edition scan | Defines lumpability for every starting distribution and the equal block-transition-sum criterion, Theorem 6.3.2. Host metadata names the course instructor; the actual work is by Kemeny and Snell. Matrix intertwining and its CRAM implications are derived in the report. |
+
+## Repository and proof provenance
+
+All relative source links in [DISPARATE_DOMAIN_CONNECTIONS.md](DISPARATE_DOMAIN_CONNECTIONS.md) refer to the inspected local NINE65 worktree based on commit `88a7bdadbf0e1f8fbfdb2b37e9843101b8db3484`. The architectural map and public-refresh status were read before interpreting source paths. No remote task contents were available, and no external source was treated as an instruction to alter repository policy.
+
+The prior research packet at `/workspace/k-elimination-audit/UNIVERSAL_THEOREM.md` and `/workspace/k-elimination-audit/CROSS_INNOVATION_MAP.md` supplies the checked universal theorem and previous ten cross-innovation entries. This document adds no Lean verification count, runtime benchmark, priority determination or cryptographic admission. It cites older domain theories to establish precise mathematical connections, not to infer that those works previously disclosed the entire CRAM architecture.
+
+## Local boundary checks
+
+A temporary Python standard-library check executed on this worktree on 12 November 2026 verified the floor-division period criterion for all 4,096 triples `1 <= c,b <= 8`, `1 <= P <= 64`. It compared the predicted condition to the actual quotient increment modulo `b` for every `x` in `0..c-1`; those increments are periodic in `x` with period `c`. It also checked all 125 received words of the length-three repetition example over `F_5`, confirming that at most one target sum can lie within one symbol error, and executed the modular/max-merge counterexample. Every relative file link in both reports resolved. These finite checks support the examples and boundary conditions; the general deductions rely on their written proofs and have not been added to Lean in this workstream.
